select public.fd_ai_upsert_chunks('fd-textbook', '825773c58568', 17, $aim_json$[{"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:17.0#1", "source_key": "fd-textbook:17.0", "chapter": 17, "section": "17.0", "heading": "Decide first", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 1, "title": "Chapter 17, Credit Derivatives and Counterparty Risk — 17.0 Decide first", "body": "⚙ Decision — choose first; the theory follows\nWhat does the market say about default?\nFive-year bonds issued by Northwind yield 1.5 percentage points more than five-year Treasury bonds. Bondholders expect to recover 40 percent of the face value of the bonds if Northwind defaults. Approximately what probability of default per year does the market assign to Northwind?"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:17.1#2", "source_key": "fd-textbook:17.1", "chapter": 17, "section": "17.1", "heading": "Credit risk and the probability of default", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 2, "title": "Chapter 17, Credit Derivatives and Counterparty Risk — 17.1 Credit risk and the probability of default", "body": "Credit risk is the risk of loss because a borrower or a counterparty fails to make a payment that it owes. The preceding chapters have assumed that every party to a contract pays what it owes. This chapter removes the assumption. It considers two questions: how the probability that a company defaults is measured, and how derivatives are used to transfer the risk of default from one party to another. It then considers the credit risk that arises in derivatives themselves, when the party on the other side of a contract fails.\nThe probability of default is described most conveniently by a hazard rate. Let \\(Q(t)\\) be the probability that a company survives, that is, does not default, until time \\(t\\). The hazard rate \\(\\lambda\\) is defined so that \\(\\lambda\\,\\Delta t\\) is the probability of default in a short interval \\(\\Delta t\\), conditional on no earlier default. The probability of surviving the interval is therefore \\(1-\\lambda\\Delta t\\), and \\(Q(t+\\Delta t)=Q(t)(1-\\lambda\\Delta t)\\). In the limit this gives \\(dQ/dt=-\\lambda Q\\), whose solution, when the hazard rate is constant, is an exponential.\nResult 17.1 — Survival and default"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:17.1#3", "source_key": "fd-textbook:17.1", "chapter": 17, "section": "17.1", "heading": "Credit risk and the probability of default", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 3, "title": "Chapter 17, Credit Derivatives and Counterparty Risk — 17.1 Credit risk and the probability of default", "body": "With a constant hazard rate \\(\\lambda\\), the probability of survival to time \\(t\\) is \\[ Q(t)=e^{-\\lambda t}, \\] and the probability of default by time \\(t\\) is \\(1-e^{-\\lambda t}\\). The unconditional probability of default between times \\(t_1\\) and \\(t_2\\) is \\(Q(t_1)-Q(t_2)=e^{-\\lambda t_1}-e^{-\\lambda t_2}\\). When the hazard rate varies with time, \\(\\lambda t\\) is replaced by \\(\\bar\\lambda(t)\\,t\\), where \\(\\bar\\lambda(t)\\) is the average hazard rate between time zero and time \\(t\\).\nTwo probabilities must be distinguished. The unconditional probability of default in the third year is the probability, as seen today, that the company defaults during that year. The conditional probability of default in the third year is the probability that it defaults during the year given that it has survived the first two. The second is the first divided by the probability of survival to the start of the year. With a constant hazard rate the conditional probability of default in each year is the same, \\(1-e^{-\\lambda}\\), and the unconditional probability declines from year to year, because fewer companies remain to default."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:17.1#4", "source_key": "fd-textbook:17.1", "chapter": 17, "section": "17.1", "heading": "Credit risk and the probability of default", "kind": "example", "ord": 4, "title": "Chapter 17, Credit Derivatives and Counterparty Risk — 17.1 Credit risk and the probability of default", "body": "Worked example 17.1 — Probabilities from a hazard rate\nThe hazard rate of a company is 2 percent per year. Find the probability that it survives five years, the unconditional probability that it defaults in the third year, and the probability of default in the third year conditional on survival for two years.\n1\nSurvival. \\(Q(5)=e^{-0.02\\times5}=e^{-0.10}=0.9048\\). The probability of default within five years is 0.0952.\n2\nUnconditional probability for the third year. \\(Q(2)-Q(3)=e^{-0.04}-e^{-0.06}=0.96079-0.94176=0.01902\\).\n3\nConditional probability. \\(0.01902/0.96079=0.01980=1-e^{-0.02}\\). It equals the conditional probability for every other year, and is slightly less than the hazard rate itself."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:17.2#5", "source_key": "fd-textbook:17.2", "chapter": 17, "section": "17.2", "heading": "Default probabilities from bond prices", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 5, "title": "Chapter 17, Credit Derivatives and Counterparty Risk — 17.2 Default probabilities from bond prices", "body": "A bond issued by a company provides a higher yield than a bond with the same cash flows issued by the government, because the holder bears the risk of default. The excess of the yield on the corporate bond over the yield on the risk-free bond is the credit spread. When a company defaults, bondholders do not usually lose everything. The recovery rate \\(R\\) is the value of a bond shortly after a default, as a proportion of its face value. Recovery rates on senior unsecured bonds have averaged about 40 percent.\nThe credit spread compensates the holder for the expected loss from default. In a short interval \\(\\Delta t\\), the probability of default is \\(\\lambda\\Delta t\\) and the proportion of the value of the bond that is lost on default is approximately \\(1-R\\). The expected loss per unit of time is \\(\\lambda(1-R)\\), and the spread must equal it.\nResult 17.2 — The hazard rate implied by a credit spread\nIf \\(s\\) is the credit spread of a bond and \\(R\\) the expected recovery rate, the average hazard rate over the life of the bond is approximately \\[ \\bar\\lambda=\\frac{s}{1-R}. \\]"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:17.2#6", "source_key": "fd-textbook:17.2", "chapter": 17, "section": "17.2", "heading": "Default probabilities from bond prices", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 6, "title": "Chapter 17, Credit Derivatives and Counterparty Risk — 17.2 Default probabilities from bond prices", "body": "This answers the opening question. A spread of 1.5 percent and a recovery rate of 40 percent imply \\(\\bar\\lambda=0.015/0.6=0.025\\), a hazard rate of 2.5 percent per year. The spread itself, 1.5 percent, is not the probability of default: it is the expected loss, which is the probability multiplied by the proportion lost. The figure 0.6 percent multiplies by the loss rather than divides."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:17.2#7", "source_key": "fd-textbook:17.2", "chapter": 17, "section": "17.2", "heading": "Real-world and risk-neutral probabilities", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 7, "title": "Chapter 17, Credit Derivatives and Counterparty Risk — 17.2 Real-world and risk-neutral probabilities", "body": "Probabilities of default can also be estimated from history. Rating agencies publish the proportion of companies with each rating that defaulted within one, five, or ten years. For companies rated A, the historical rate of default has been roughly one tenth of the rate implied by their bond spreads. The two estimates differ because they answer different questions. The estimate from bond prices is a risk-neutral probability: it is the probability that values the bond correctly when expected cash flows are discounted at the risk-free rate. Investors require compensation for bearing credit risk beyond the expected loss. Defaults cluster in recessions, when losses are hardest to bear, and the risk cannot be diversified away. Corporate bonds are also less liquid than government bonds. These premiums are part of the spread, and Result 17.2 attributes them to default."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:17.2#8", "source_key": "fd-textbook:17.2", "chapter": 17, "section": "17.2", "heading": "Real-world and risk-neutral probabilities", "kind": "warning", "ord": 8, "title": "Chapter 17, Credit Derivatives and Counterparty Risk — 17.2 Real-world and risk-neutral probabilities", "body": "Common error\nThe two probabilities have different uses, and they should not be exchanged. The risk-neutral probability is the correct one for valuing a credit derivative, because valuation proceeds by Principle 10.1, as for every other derivative in this book. The real-world probability is the correct one for estimating the losses that a portfolio will actually suffer, as in the calculation of value at risk in Chapter 13. A bank that used risk-neutral probabilities to forecast its credit losses would greatly overstate them; one that used real-world probabilities to price credit protection would sell it far too cheaply."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:17.2#9", "source_key": "fd-textbook:17.2", "chapter": 17, "section": "17.2", "heading": "Real-world and risk-neutral probabilities", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 9, "title": "Chapter 17, Credit Derivatives and Counterparty Risk — 17.2 Real-world and risk-neutral probabilities", "body": "Concept check 17.1\nA company's bonds yield 200 basis points over Treasury bonds, and the expected recovery rate is 50 percent. Its historical default rate, for companies of its rating, is 0.5 percent per year. Which statement is correct?"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:17.3#10", "source_key": "fd-textbook:17.3", "chapter": 17, "section": "17.3", "heading": "Credit default swaps", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 10, "title": "Chapter 17, Credit Derivatives and Counterparty Risk — 17.3 Credit default swaps", "body": "A credit default swap (CDS) is a contract that provides insurance against the default of a particular company, the reference entity. The buyer of protection makes periodic payments to the seller of protection until the end of the life of the swap or until a default by the reference entity, whichever is earlier. If the reference entity defaults, the seller pays the buyer the loss on a specified amount of the reference entity's bonds, the notional principal. The annual payment, as a proportion of the notional principal, is the CDS spread."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:17.3#11", "source_key": "fd-textbook:17.3", "chapter": 17, "section": "17.3", "heading": "Credit default swaps", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 11, "title": "Chapter 17, Credit Derivatives and Counterparty Risk — 17.3 Credit default swaps", "body": "A default, called a credit event, includes failure to make a payment when it is due, bankruptcy, and certain restructurings of debt. When a credit event occurs, the swap is settled in one of two ways. In physical settlement the buyer delivers bonds of the reference entity with a face value equal to the notional principal and receives the face value in cash. In cash settlement the seller pays the notional principal multiplied by \\(1-R\\), where \\(R\\) is the recovery rate, the price of the bonds per dollar of face value shortly after the event. Since 2009 the recovery rate has been set, for most swaps, by an auction organized by the industry among dealers a few weeks after the credit event. The buyer also pays the part of the periodic payment that has accrued since the last payment date, since protection was provided for that period."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:17.3#12", "source_key": "fd-textbook:17.3", "chapter": 17, "section": "17.3", "heading": "Credit default swaps", "kind": "example", "ord": 12, "title": "Chapter 17, Credit Derivatives and Counterparty Risk — 17.3 Credit default swaps", "body": "Worked example 17.2 — Settlement on default\nA pension fund buys five-year protection on Northwind with a notional principal of $10 million, at a CDS spread of 120 basis points, payable quarterly in arrears. Northwind defaults two years and one month after the swap begins. The auction sets the recovery rate at 35 percent. State all the cash flows.\n1\nPayments before the default. Each quarterly payment is \\(0.0120\\times10{,}000{,}000\\times0.25=\\$30{,}000\\). Eight payments are made in the first two years, a total of $240,000.\n2\nAccrued payment. One month of protection has been provided since the last payment date. The buyer pays \\(0.0120\\times10{,}000{,}000\\times\\tfrac{1}{12}=\\$10{,}000\\).\n3\nPayment by the seller. \\((1-0.35)\\times10{,}000{,}000=\\$6{,}500{,}000\\). Under physical settlement the fund would instead deliver $10 million of face value of Northwind bonds, worth $3.5 million, and receive $10 million, with the same result.\n4\nResult. A fund that held $10 million of Northwind bonds and the swap has, in effect, converted its holding into a risk-free bond for an annual cost of 1.2 percent. No further payments are made."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:17.3#13", "source_key": "fd-textbook:17.3", "chapter": 17, "section": "17.3", "heading": "Credit default swaps", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 13, "title": "Chapter 17, Credit Derivatives and Counterparty Risk — 17.3 Credit default swaps", "body": "A CDS resembles insurance, but it differs in one important respect: the buyer need not own the bonds of the reference entity. A buyer that does not is speculating on a deterioration in the credit of the company, as a holder of a put option speculates on a fall in a share price. The notional principal of swaps on a reference entity can therefore exceed the bonds that it has issued. Physical settlement then obliges buyers to acquire bonds in order to deliver them, which can drive up the price of the bonds after a default; the auction was introduced in part to avoid this."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:17.3#14", "source_key": "fd-textbook:17.3", "chapter": 17, "section": "17.3", "heading": "Key terms", "kind": "term", "ord": 14, "title": "Chapter 17, Credit Derivatives and Counterparty Risk — 17.3 Key terms", "body": "Key terms: Reference entity: the company whose default the swap insures. Credit event: a default, as defined in the contract. Notional principal: the face value of bonds covered. CDS spread: the annual payment for protection, as a proportion of the notional principal. Recovery rate: the value of a bond shortly after default, per dollar of face value. Cheapest-to-deliver bond: under physical settlement, the bond of lowest value that the buyer may deliver."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:17.4#15", "source_key": "fd-textbook:17.4", "chapter": 17, "section": "17.4", "heading": "The valuation of a credit default swap", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 15, "title": "Chapter 17, Credit Derivatives and Counterparty Risk — 17.4 The valuation of a credit default swap", "body": "A CDS is valued, as other swaps are, by computing the present values of its two sets of cash flows in a risk-neutral world. The premium leg consists of the payments by the buyer; the protection leg consists of the payment by the seller in the event of default. At the start of the swap the spread is set so that the two have the same present value.\nConsider a swap with a life of \\(T\\) years and annual payments at the end of each year, and suppose that a default can occur only in the middle of a year. Let \\(Q_i\\) be the probability of survival to the end of year \\(i\\), so that \\(Q_{i-1}-Q_i\\) is the probability of default during year \\(i\\), and let \\(P(t)=e^{-rt}\\) be the price of a risk-free zero-coupon bond paying $1 at time \\(t\\). Per dollar of notional principal and per unit of spread, the expected present value of the regular payments is \\(\\sum Q_iP(i)\\), and that of the accrued payments on default is \\(\\sum(Q_{i-1}-Q_i)\\tfrac12P(i-\\tfrac12)\\). Their sum, \\(A\\), is the risky annuity or risky duration of the swap. The expected present value of the protection leg is \\((1-R)\\sum(Q_{i-1}-Q_i)P(i-\\tfrac12)\\).\nResult 17.3 — The CDS spread"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:17.4#16", "source_key": "fd-textbook:17.4", "chapter": 17, "section": "17.4", "heading": "The valuation of a credit default swap", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 16, "title": "Chapter 17, Credit Derivatives and Counterparty Risk — 17.4 The valuation of a credit default swap", "body": "The spread that gives the two legs equal value is \\[ s=\\frac{(1-R)\\sum_{i=1}^{T}(Q_{i-1}-Q_i)\\,P(i-\\tfrac12)}{\\sum_{i=1}^{T}\\left[Q_iP(i)+\\tfrac12(Q_{i-1}-Q_i)P(i-\\tfrac12)\\right]}. \\] When payments and defaults may occur at any time and the hazard rate is constant, the spread is exactly \\[ s=\\lambda(1-R). \\]\nThe second expression is the relation of Result 17.2 read in reverse, and it is sometimes called the credit triangle: any two of the spread, the hazard rate, and the recovery rate determine the third. It is the reason that CDS spreads, like bond spreads, are used to estimate risk-neutral hazard rates. The spreads of swaps are the more reliable source, since they are quoted directly for standard maturities and do not depend on the coupon or the liquidity of a particular bond."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:17.4#17", "source_key": "fd-textbook:17.4", "chapter": 17, "section": "17.4", "heading": "The valuation of a credit default swap", "kind": "example", "ord": 17, "title": "Chapter 17, Credit Derivatives and Counterparty Risk — 17.4 The valuation of a credit default swap", "body": "Worked example 17.3 — Computing the spread\nFind the spread of a five-year CDS with annual payments when the hazard rate is 2 percent, the recovery rate is 40 percent, and the risk-free rate is 5 percent with continuous compounding.\n1\nProbabilities.\nYear12345\nSurvival \\(Q_i\\)0.98020.96080.94180.92310.9048\nDefault in year0.01980.01940.01900.01860.0183\n2\nPremium leg. \\(\\sum Q_iP(i)=0.9802e^{-0.05}+\\dots+0.9048e^{-0.25}=4.0728\\). The accrued payments add \\(\\sum(Q_{i-1}-Q_i)\\tfrac12P(i-\\tfrac12)=0.0422\\). The risky annuity is \\(A=4.1150\\).\n3\nProtection leg. \\(0.6\\sum(Q_{i-1}-Q_i)P(i-\\tfrac12)=0.6\\times0.08436=0.05062\\).\n4\nSpread. \\(s=0.05062/4.1150=0.0123\\), or 123 basis points. The approximation \\(\\lambda(1-R)=0.02\\times0.6\\) gives 120 basis points; the difference arises from annual payments and the assumption that defaults occur in mid-year."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:17.4#18", "source_key": "fd-textbook:17.4", "chapter": 17, "section": "17.4", "heading": "Marking a swap to market", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 18, "title": "Chapter 17, Credit Derivatives and Counterparty Risk — 17.4 Marking a swap to market", "body": "Once a swap has been agreed, its spread is fixed, and the spread quoted in the market for new swaps on the same entity changes as the credit of the entity changes. The value of the swap changes accordingly. A buyer of protection at spread \\(s_0\\) can offset the swap by selling protection at the current spread \\(s\\) for the remaining life. The protection legs then cancel, and the buyer receives \\(s-s_0\\) per year for as long as the entity survives.\nResult 17.4 — The value of an existing CDS\nThe value to the buyer of protection of a swap with spread \\(s_0\\) and notional principal \\(L\\) is \\[ (s-s_0)\\,A\\,L, \\] where \\(s\\) is the current spread for the remaining life and \\(A\\) is the risky annuity for that life. The value to the seller is the negative of this."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:17.4#19", "source_key": "fd-textbook:17.4", "chapter": 17, "section": "17.4", "heading": "Marking a swap to market", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 19, "title": "Chapter 17, Credit Derivatives and Counterparty Risk — 17.4 Marking a swap to market", "body": "Suppose that the pension fund of Worked example 17.2 had bought protection at 100 basis points, and that the market spread is now 123 basis points with a risky annuity of 4.115. The swap is worth \\((0.0123-0.0100)\\times4.115\\times10{,}000{,}000\\), about $95,000, to the fund. Since 2009 standard swaps in North America have paid a fixed spread of 100 or 500 basis points, and the difference between the fixed and the market spread is settled at the start by an upfront payment of this amount."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:17.4#20", "source_key": "fd-textbook:17.4", "chapter": 17, "section": "17.4", "heading": "Marking a swap to market", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 20, "title": "Chapter 17, Credit Derivatives and Counterparty Risk — 17.4 Marking a swap to market", "body": "⚙ Interactive figure 17.1 — Survival, default, and the CDS spread\nFrom the hazard rate to the price of protection\nThe figure shows the probability that the reference entity survives, and the probability that it has defaulted, over ten years for a constant hazard rate. Vary the hazard rate and the recovery rate. The readout gives the spread of a five-year swap with annual payments, computed by Result 17.3 with a risk-free rate of 5 percent, and the value of an existing swap bought at 100 basis points."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:17.4#21", "source_key": "fd-textbook:17.4", "chapter": 17, "section": "17.4", "heading": "Marking a swap to market", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 21, "title": "Chapter 17, Credit Derivatives and Counterparty Risk — 17.4 Marking a swap to market", "body": "The figure shows two properties of the spread. It is nearly proportional to the hazard rate. And it falls as the recovery rate rises, since the protection seller then pays less when default occurs: with a recovery rate of 80 percent, a hazard rate of 2 percent supports a spread of only about 41 basis points. A spread alone does not identify the probability of default; the recovery rate must be assumed. Practitioners usually assume 40 percent.\nConcept check 17.2\nThe five-year CDS spread of a company rises from 150 to 300 basis points, and the recovery rate is assumed to remain 40 percent. Which statement is correct?"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:17.5#22", "source_key": "fd-textbook:17.5", "chapter": 17, "section": "17.5", "heading": "Credit indices, total return swaps, and collateralized debt obligations", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 22, "title": "Chapter 17, Credit Derivatives and Counterparty Risk — 17.5 Credit indices, total return swaps, and collateralized debt obligations", "body": "A credit index tracks the CDS spreads of a portfolio of companies. The two principal families are CDX, which covers North America, and iTraxx, which covers Europe and Asia. The CDX North American Investment Grade index, for example, comprises 125 companies with investment-grade ratings, and its composition is revised every six months. A swap on the index provides protection on all 125 companies at once. If the notional principal is $125 million, each company is covered for $1 million; when one defaults, the seller pays the loss on $1 million, and the notional principal and the payments are reduced by one 125th. The quoted index is approximately the average of the CDS spreads of the companies in it."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:17.5#23", "source_key": "fd-textbook:17.5", "chapter": 17, "section": "17.5", "heading": "Credit indices, total return swaps, and collateralized debt obligations", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 23, "title": "Chapter 17, Credit Derivatives and Counterparty Risk — 17.5 Credit indices, total return swaps, and collateralized debt obligations", "body": "A total return swap exchanges the total return on an asset, which is its income and the change in its value, for a floating rate of interest plus a spread. The payer of the total return on a corporate bond receives LIBOR or its successor rate plus a spread, and pays the coupons on the bond and any increase in its price; the receiver pays the floating rate and any decrease in the price. The receiver obtains the economic exposure of owning the bond without buying it. In effect, the payer has lent to the receiver the cost of the bond and holds the bond as collateral. Total return swaps on shares permit an investor to take large positions without owning the shares, a point taken up in Chapter 18."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:17.5#24", "source_key": "fd-textbook:17.5", "chapter": 17, "section": "17.5", "heading": "Credit indices, total return swaps, and collateralized debt obligations", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 24, "title": "Chapter 17, Credit Derivatives and Counterparty Risk — 17.5 Credit indices, total return swaps, and collateralized debt obligations", "body": "A collateralized debt obligation (CDO) redistributes the credit risk of a portfolio of debt among investors in several classes, or tranches. In a simple structure the equity tranche bears the first 5 percent of losses on the portfolio; the mezzanine tranche bears losses between 5 and 20 percent; and the senior tranche bears losses above 20 percent. Each tranche receives a rate of interest that compensates for its risk: the equity tranche a high rate and the senior tranche a low one. In a synthetic CDO the portfolio is a set of credit default swaps rather than bonds."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:17.5#25", "source_key": "fd-textbook:17.5", "chapter": 17, "section": "17.5", "heading": "Credit indices, total return swaps, and collateralized debt obligations", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 25, "title": "Chapter 17, Credit Derivatives and Counterparty Risk — 17.5 Credit indices, total return swaps, and collateralized debt obligations", "body": "The risk of a tranche depends critically on the default correlation among the companies in the portfolio, which is the tendency of defaults to occur together. When correlation is low, defaults are spread over time and large losses on the whole portfolio are very improbable, so that the senior tranche is nearly free of risk. When correlation is high, defaults come together, and large losses become more likely: the senior tranche becomes riskier, and the equity tranche, paradoxically, safer, since there is a greater chance that few defaults occur at all. The standard model of the market, the one-factor Gaussian copula, summarizes the correlation in a single parameter. Underestimates of default correlation in tranches backed by mortgages were central to the losses of 2007 and 2008, when many senior tranches with the highest rating lost most of their value."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:17.5#26", "source_key": "fd-textbook:17.5", "chapter": 17, "section": "17.5", "heading": "Credit indices, total return swaps, and collateralized debt obligations", "kind": "notes", "ord": 26, "title": "Chapter 17, Credit Derivatives and Counterparty Risk — 17.5 Credit indices, total return swaps, and collateralized debt obligations", "body": "Historical note\nAmerican International Group, through its Financial Products subsidiary, sold protection on several hundred billion dollars of senior tranches backed by mortgages, in the belief that they would never suffer losses. Its contracts required it to post collateral if their value moved against it or if its own credit rating was lowered. Both occurred in September 2008, and the company could not meet the demands for collateral. The United States government provided support that eventually exceeded $180 billion. The same month, the bankruptcy of Lehman Brothers became the largest credit event in the market: the auction to settle swaps on Lehman set a recovery rate of 8.625 percent, so that sellers of protection paid more than 91 cents on each dollar of notional principal."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:17.6#27", "source_key": "fd-textbook:17.6", "chapter": 17, "section": "17.6", "heading": "Counterparty credit risk", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 27, "title": "Chapter 17, Credit Derivatives and Counterparty Risk — 17.6 Counterparty credit risk", "body": "A derivative traded over the counter exposes each party to the risk that the other will default. The exposure is unlike that of a loan. The amount at risk is not fixed: a party loses on the default of its counterparty only if the derivative then has a positive value to it, and the value varies over time. If the value is negative, the defaulting counterparty's administrators require that the amount owed be paid in full, and the surviving party gains nothing. The exposure at time \\(t\\) is therefore \\(\\max(V_t,0)\\), where \\(V_t\\) is the value of the derivative to the party. The expected exposure \\(EE(t)\\) is the mean of the exposure in a risk-neutral world.\nResult 17.5 — The credit value adjustment\nDivide the life of a derivative into intervals, and let \\(q_i\\) be the risk-neutral probability that the counterparty defaults in the \\(i\\)th interval, \\(t_i\\) its midpoint, and \\(R\\) the recovery rate of the counterparty. The credit value adjustment is \\[ \\text{CVA}=(1-R)\\sum_i q_i\\,EE(t_i)\\,P(t_i), \\] and the value of the derivative allowing for the default of the counterparty is the value without default less the CVA."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:17.6#28", "source_key": "fd-textbook:17.6", "chapter": 17, "section": "17.6", "heading": "Counterparty credit risk", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 28, "title": "Chapter 17, Credit Derivatives and Counterparty Risk — 17.6 Counterparty credit risk", "body": "The expected exposure depends on the type of derivative. For an option that a party has bought, the value is never negative, and the exposure is the value of the option itself. Since the expected present value of the option's future value is its value today, \\(f_0\\), the CVA reduces to \\((1-R)f_0\\) multiplied by the probability that the counterparty defaults during the life of the option. For an option that a party has sold, the exposure is zero. For a forward contract the exposure grows with time, since the distribution of the value of the contract widens as the delivery date approaches. For an interest rate swap it rises and then falls: the uncertainty about rates increases with time, but the number of payments remaining declines, and the expected exposure is greatest about one third of the way through the life of the swap."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:17.6#29", "source_key": "fd-textbook:17.6", "chapter": 17, "section": "17.6", "heading": "Counterparty credit risk", "kind": "example", "ord": 29, "title": "Chapter 17, Credit Derivatives and Counterparty Risk — 17.6 Counterparty credit risk", "body": "Worked example 17.4 — The CVA of a purchased option\nNorthwind buys from a dealer a two-year option, settled at expiration, whose value without default is $5 million. The dealer's hazard rate is 2 percent and its recovery rate 40 percent. Find the CVA and the value of the option.\n1\nProbability of default. \\(1-e^{-0.02\\times2}=0.0392\\).\n2\nCVA. \\((1-0.4)\\times5{,}000{,}000\\times0.0392=\\$117{,}600\\).\n3\nValue. \\(5{,}000{,}000-117{,}600=\\$4{,}882{,}400\\). Northwind should pay no more than this for the option from this dealer; an option from a dealer of better credit is worth more."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:17.6#30", "source_key": "fd-textbook:17.6", "chapter": 17, "section": "17.6", "heading": "Counterparty credit risk", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 30, "title": "Chapter 17, Credit Derivatives and Counterparty Risk — 17.6 Counterparty credit risk", "body": "The default of the party itself is the mirror image. A derivative with a negative value to a party is a liability, and the party's own default would relieve it of part of the liability. The debit value adjustment (DVA) is computed as the CVA is, with the party's own probability of default and the expected value of \\(\\max(-V_t,0)\\). The value of the derivative allowing for the default of both parties is the value without default less the CVA plus the DVA. Accounting standards require banks to report DVA, with the uncomfortable consequence that a bank whose credit deteriorates reports a gain."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:17.6#31", "source_key": "fd-textbook:17.6", "chapter": 17, "section": "17.6", "heading": "Netting and collateral", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 31, "title": "Chapter 17, Credit Derivatives and Counterparty Risk — 17.6 Netting and collateral", "body": "Two arrangements greatly reduce counterparty risk. Under netting, all the derivatives between two parties are treated as a single contract if one of them defaults: the surviving party's exposure is \\(\\max(\\sum V_j,0)\\), the positive part of the total value, rather than \\(\\sum\\max(V_j,0)\\). A party that has contracts with a counterparty worth $10 million and −$8 million has an exposure of $10 million without netting and $2 million with it. Under a collateral agreement, a party to whom the portfolio has a negative value must post cash or securities to the other, adjusted each day as the value changes. The exposure is then limited to the change in value over the few days that would elapse between the counterparty's last posting of collateral and the closing out of its positions. Contracts cleared through a central counterparty, as most standard swaps now must be, are subject to daily margin like futures, and their counterparty risk is that of the clearing house."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:17.6#32", "source_key": "fd-textbook:17.6", "chapter": 17, "section": "17.6", "heading": "Netting and collateral", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 32, "title": "Chapter 17, Credit Derivatives and Counterparty Risk — 17.6 Netting and collateral", "body": "⚙ Interactive figure 17.2 — Expected exposure and the CVA\nA five-year forward and a five-year swap with the same counterparty\nThe figure shows the expected exposure of a dealer to a counterparty, with no collateral, over the life of a five-year contract with a notional principal of $100 million: a forward contract on an asset with a volatility of 20 percent, or an interest rate swap on a rate whose volatility is one percentage point per year. Vary the counterparty's hazard rate and recovery rate. The readout gives the CVA computed by Result 17.5 with quarterly intervals and a risk-free rate of 5 percent."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:17.6#33", "source_key": "fd-textbook:17.6", "chapter": 17, "section": "17.6", "heading": "Netting and collateral", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 33, "title": "Chapter 17, Credit Derivatives and Counterparty Risk — 17.6 Netting and collateral", "body": "With a hazard rate of 2 percent and a recovery rate of 40 percent, the CVA of the forward contract is about $582,000 and that of the swap about $62,000. The swap carries much less counterparty risk for the same notional principal, because only the differences between interest payments are exchanged, and the principal is never paid. The CVA rises nearly in proportion to the hazard rate and to \\(1-R\\), as the credit triangle would suggest."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:17.6#34", "source_key": "fd-textbook:17.6", "chapter": 17, "section": "17.6", "heading": "Netting and collateral", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 34, "title": "Chapter 17, Credit Derivatives and Counterparty Risk — 17.6 Netting and collateral", "body": "⚙ Interactive figure 17.3 — Three-dimensional view\nSurvival probability over the hazard rate and time\nThe surface is the probability that a company survives to a given time, for hazard rates from zero to 10 percent per year and times up to ten years. Select the probability of survival, or the unconditional probability of default in each year. The gold curve is the selected time, and the marker the selected hazard rate. Drag to rotate; scroll or pinch to zoom.\nTable equivalent of this figure"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:17.6#35", "source_key": "fd-textbook:17.6", "chapter": 17, "section": "17.6", "heading": "Netting and collateral", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 35, "title": "Chapter 17, Credit Derivatives and Counterparty Risk — 17.6 Netting and collateral", "body": "Concept check 17.3\nA bank has three uncollateralized derivatives with a counterparty, worth +$7 million, −$4 million, and +$2 million to the bank. What is the bank's exposure under a netting agreement?"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:17.7#36", "source_key": "fd-textbook:17.7", "chapter": 17, "section": "17.7", "heading": "★ Derivation of the credit triangle", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 36, "title": "Chapter 17, Credit Derivatives and Counterparty Risk — 17.7 ★ Derivation of the credit triangle", "body": "Optional section. This section obtains the exact relation \\(s=\\lambda(1-R)\\) of Result 17.3 for a swap with continuous payments, and shows why the same relation describes the spread of a bond.\nLet the hazard rate \\(\\lambda\\) and the risk-free rate \\(r\\) be constant. The probability of survival to time \\(t\\) is \\(e^{-\\lambda t}\\), and the probability density of default at time \\(t\\) is \\(\\lambda e^{-\\lambda t}\\). Per dollar of notional principal, the buyer pays at the rate \\(s\\) for as long as the entity survives. The expected present value of the premium leg is \\[ \\int_0^T s\\,e^{-\\lambda t}e^{-rt}\\,dt=s\\,\\frac{1-e^{-(r+\\lambda)T}}{r+\\lambda}. \\] No accrued payment arises, because the payments are continuous. The seller pays \\(1-R\\) at the time of default. The expected present value of the protection leg is \\[ \\int_0^T (1-R)\\,\\lambda e^{-\\lambda t}e^{-rt}\\,dt=(1-R)\\lambda\\,\\frac{1-e^{-(r+\\lambda)T}}{r+\\lambda}. \\]"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:17.7#37", "source_key": "fd-textbook:17.7", "chapter": 17, "section": "17.7", "heading": "★ Derivation of the credit triangle", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 37, "title": "Chapter 17, Credit Derivatives and Counterparty Risk — 17.7 ★ Derivation of the credit triangle", "body": "The two integrals have the same factor, \\([1-e^{-(r+\\lambda)T}]/(r+\\lambda)\\), which is the risky annuity \\(A\\) in continuous form. Setting the two legs equal gives \\(s=\\lambda(1-R)\\), whatever the maturity and whatever the risk-free rate. When the hazard rate varies with time, the factors differ, and the spread is a weighted average of \\(\\lambda(t)(1-R)\\) over the life of the swap.\nThe same argument applies to a bond. Consider a floating-rate bond of the reference entity that pays the risk-free rate plus a spread \\(s\\), and suppose that on default the holder receives \\(R\\) per dollar of face value. A portfolio of the bond and a CDS on it is free of risk: the CDS restores the face value on default. The portfolio must therefore earn the risk-free rate, so that the spread on the bond equals the CDS spread, \\(s=\\lambda(1-R)\\). This is Result 17.2. In practice the two spreads differ by the CDS–bond basis, which arises from the cost of funding a position in the bond, the cheapest-to-deliver option, and the counterparty risk of the CDS itself. The basis was usually small before 2008 and became large and negative in the crisis, when the funds needed to exploit it could not be borrowed."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:17.summary#38", "source_key": "fd-textbook:17.summary", "chapter": 17, "section": "17.summary", "heading": "Summary", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 38, "title": "Chapter 17, Credit Derivatives and Counterparty Risk — 17.summary Summary", "body": "With a constant hazard rate \\(\\lambda\\), the probability of survival to time \\(t\\) is \\(e^{-\\lambda t}\\). The probability of default in a year conditional on survival to its start is constant; the unconditional probability declines.\nThe credit spread of a bond is the expected loss per year in a risk-neutral world, so that the average hazard rate is approximately \\(s/(1-R)\\). Risk-neutral probabilities of default exceed real-world probabilities, because investors require a premium for bearing credit risk. The first are used for valuation and the second for forecasting losses.\nA credit default swap provides protection against the default of a reference entity in return for periodic payments at the CDS spread. On a credit event the seller pays the notional principal multiplied by one minus the recovery rate, which is usually set by auction.\nThe CDS spread equates the expected present values of the premium leg and the protection leg; with a constant hazard rate it is \\(\\lambda(1-R)\\). An existing swap is worth \\((s-s_0)AL\\) to the buyer of protection, where \\(A\\) is the risky annuity."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:17.summary#39", "source_key": "fd-textbook:17.summary", "chapter": 17, "section": "17.summary", "heading": "Summary", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 39, "title": "Chapter 17, Credit Derivatives and Counterparty Risk — 17.summary Summary", "body": "Credit indices provide protection on a portfolio of companies. A total return swap transfers the whole return on an asset. A CDO divides the credit risk of a portfolio among tranches, whose risk depends critically on default correlation.\nThe credit value adjustment of a derivative is \\((1-R)\\sum q_iEE(t_i)P(t_i)\\). Expected exposure is the value of a purchased option, grows with time for a forward, and rises and falls for a swap. Netting and collateral reduce exposure greatly."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:17.exercises#40", "source_key": "fd-textbook:17.exercises", "chapter": 17, "section": "17.exercises", "heading": "Exercises", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 40, "title": "Chapter 17, Credit Derivatives and Counterparty Risk — 17.exercises Exercises", "body": "17.1 The hazard rate of a company is 3 percent per year. Find (a) the probability that it survives three years; (b) the unconditional probability that it defaults in the third year; (c) the probability that it defaults in the third year given that it has survived two years.\n(a) \\(e^{-0.09}=0.9139\\). (b) \\(e^{-0.06}-e^{-0.09}=0.9418-0.9139=0.0278\\). (c) \\(0.0278/0.9418=0.0296=1-e^{-0.03}\\).\n17.2 The spread of a company's five-year bonds over Treasury bonds is 240 basis points. Estimate the average hazard rate over five years if the recovery rate is (a) 40 percent; (b) 20 percent. Explain why the estimate is lower when the assumed recovery rate is lower.\n(a) \\(0.024/0.6=4.0\\) percent. (b) \\(0.024/0.8=3.0\\) percent. The estimate falls as the recovery rate falls: when more is lost on each default, fewer defaults are needed to produce the same expected loss.\n17.3 The risk-neutral hazard rate of a reference entity is 1.5 percent and the recovery rate 30 percent. Estimate the CDS spread. If the spread rises to 210 basis points while the recovery rate is unchanged, what hazard rate does the market now imply?"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:17.exercises#41", "source_key": "fd-textbook:17.exercises", "chapter": 17, "section": "17.exercises", "heading": "Exercises", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 41, "title": "Chapter 17, Credit Derivatives and Counterparty Risk — 17.exercises Exercises", "body": "\\(s=0.015\\times0.7=0.0105\\), or 105 basis points. At 210 basis points, \\(\\lambda=0.021/0.7=3.0\\) percent: the spread and the hazard rate have both doubled.\n17.4 A fund buys protection on a reference entity with a notional principal of $20 million at a spread of 90 basis points, paid quarterly. The entity defaults one month after a payment date, and the auction sets a recovery rate of 42 percent. Find the payment by the seller and the accrued payment by the buyer.\nThe seller pays \\((1-0.42)\\times20{,}000{,}000=\\$11{,}600{,}000\\). The buyer pays \\(0.009\\times20{,}000{,}000\\times\\tfrac1{12}=\\$15{,}000\\) for the month of protection since the last payment.\n17.5 A bank bought five-year protection with a notional principal of $10 million at 80 basis points. The spread for the remaining life is now 130 basis points and the risky annuity is 4.2. Find the value of the swap to the bank. Has the credit of the reference entity improved or deteriorated?\n\\((0.0130-0.0080)\\times4.2\\times10{,}000{,}000=\\$210{,}000\\). The swap is an asset to the buyer of protection. The credit has deteriorated: protection now costs more than the bank pays for it."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:17.exercises#42", "source_key": "fd-textbook:17.exercises", "chapter": 17, "section": "17.exercises", "heading": "Exercises", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 42, "title": "Chapter 17, Credit Derivatives and Counterparty Risk — 17.exercises Exercises", "body": "17.6 A dealer has four uncollateralized derivatives with a counterparty, worth +$12 million, −$5 million, +$3 million, and −$9 million to the dealer. Find the dealer's exposure (a) without netting; (b) with netting.\n(a) The sum of the positive values, \\(12+3=\\$15\\) million. (b) \\(\\max(12-5+3-9,0)=\\$1\\) million. Netting has reduced the exposure by more than 90 percent.\n17.7 A company buys a three-year option from a dealer. Its value without default is $2 million. The dealer's hazard rate is 1 percent and its recovery rate 30 percent. Find the CVA. What is the CVA of the dealer's position in the same option?\nThe probability that the dealer defaults within three years is \\(1-e^{-0.03}=0.02955\\). The CVA is \\(0.7\\times2{,}000{,}000\\times0.02955=\\$41{,}400\\). The dealer has sold the option, whose value to it is never positive, so that its exposure to the company, and its CVA, are zero. The company's possible default affects the dealer only through its DVA, which is likewise zero, as the company owes nothing."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:17.exercises#43", "source_key": "fd-textbook:17.exercises", "chapter": 17, "section": "17.exercises", "heading": "Exercises", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 43, "title": "Chapter 17, Credit Derivatives and Counterparty Risk — 17.exercises Exercises", "body": "17.8 The historical rate of default of companies rated A is about 0.1 percent per year, while their bond spreads imply a hazard rate of about 1 percent. Give three reasons for the difference, and state which estimate a bank should use (a) to price protection that it sells; (b) to estimate the losses on its loan portfolio in a stress test.\nInvestors require compensation beyond the expected loss because defaults are correlated with one another and with recessions, so that the risk cannot be diversified; corporate bonds are less liquid than Treasury bonds; and the risk-free benchmark used by traders is below the Treasury rate, which raises measured spreads. (a) The risk-neutral estimate, since pricing follows risk-neutral valuation. (b) The real-world estimate, adjusted for the stress scenario, since the purpose is to forecast actual losses."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:17.further-reading#44", "source_key": "fd-textbook:17.further-reading", "chapter": 17, "section": "17.further-reading", "heading": "Further reading", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 44, "title": "Chapter 17, Credit Derivatives and Counterparty Risk — 17.further-reading Further reading", "body": "Duffie, D., and Singleton, K. J. (2003). Credit Risk: Pricing, Measurement, and Management. Princeton University Press.\nGregory, J. (2020). The xVA Challenge: Counterparty Risk, Funding, Collateral, Capital and Initial Margin (4th ed.). Wiley.\nHull, J. C. (2022). Options, Futures, and Other Derivatives (11th ed.). Pearson. Chapters 24–25.\nHull, J., Predescu, M., and White, A. (2005). Bond prices, default probabilities, and risk premiums. Journal of Credit Risk, 1(2), 53–60.\nLi, D. X. (2000). On default correlation: A copula function approach. Journal of Fixed Income, 9(4), 43–54.\nMerton, R. C. (1974). On the pricing of corporate debt: The risk structure of interest rates. Journal of Finance, 29(2), 449–470."}]$aim_json$::jsonb, 'Financial Derivatives (MacroNations textbook)');
